; BUILT WITH `-checked` -- `go run ./cmd/build -checked …`.
;
; The map read's value range is derived SYNTACTICALLY, from the inserts, and
; `(go.* i 10)` is computed rather than literal -- so it is correctly refused,
; exactly as a buffer's element range refuses a computed store. Deriving it
; from the interval analysis instead is a real extension and is not made.
;
; A DYNAMIC MAP on the native Go target (maps.md).
;
; The static half -- `((map (1 10)) 1)` -- reduces away entirely and reaches no
; backend. This is the other half: a map built at run time, inserted into in a
; loop, and read with a key the compiler cannot know.
;
; `(m k)` is `(option V)`, and the `case` is what discharges the domain
; condition -- the only thing that can, because `k in dom m` is set membership
; and no decidable fragment we have decides it. That is F2, and it REMOVES an
; obligation rather than adding one: the program says what happens when the key
; is absent.
;
; It emits as Go's own comma-ok, so the option is not a thing we add -- it is a
; thing the host already has and we were discarding.
;
; The capacity is a declaration and not a hint (maps.md §6): windows ships no
; map and a growing hash table cannot be expressed, so every target gets the
; same bound rather than three growing and one not.

(use go)
(use go/fmt)
(export main)

; A DYNAMIC map: built, inserted into, and read with a key the compiler cannot
; know. `(m k)` is `(option V)` and the `case` is what discharges the domain
; condition -- the only thing that can, since `k in dom m` is set membership.
(def build3 (fn (n)
  (build-map 8 (fn (m)
    (loop ((m m) (i 0))
      (go.>= i n)  m
      else         (again (insert m i (go.* i 10)) (go.+ i 1)))))))

(def look (fn (m k) (case (m k) (some v) v none -1)))

(def main (fn ()
  (let (build3 5) (fn (m)
    (fmt.Println (go.+ (go.* 100 (look m 3)) (look m 9)))))))
